00:20:52.740 --> 00:20:54.080
And he's like, backpedal, backpedal.

526
00:20:54.260 --> 00:20:54.600
Yeah.

527
00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:59.660
And then when Michael goes to ask Daryl and Oscar's opinion about this, they're gone.

528
00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:00.760
They've exited.

529
00:21:01.100 --> 00:21:09.000
There is a tiny moment before that where over Michael's shoulder, you just see Oscar clock, what's going on.

530
00:21:09.140 --> 00:21:10.180
And it's perfect.

531
00:21:10.360 --> 00:21:12.800
It is so, so good.

532
00:21:14.300 --> 00:21:16.500
We're going to travel now to Gabe's apartment.

533
00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:18.020
It's the evening.

534
00:21:18.440 --> 00:21:20.240
Erin is giving Michael a tour.

535
00:21:20.500 --> 00:21:22.360
She's so enamored with Gabe's apartment.

536
00:21:22.620 --> 00:21:24.160
It's like a fancy grown-up apartment.

537
00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:25.900
There's big wine glasses, Jenna.

538
00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:27.600
Very large wine glasses.

539
00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:29.140
Which Phyllis is going to enjoy.

540
00:21:29.140 --> 00:21:35.920
I had a fun background catch at this entrance moment at four minutes and 45 seconds.

541
00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:41.300
You start by seeing an exterior shot of the building, and you hear a door close.

542
00:21:41.580 --> 00:21:47.220
And then it cuts to Aaron and Michael, and the apartment door is already closed.

543
00:21:47.720 --> 00:21:51.680
It says, if they'd entered, but that way we didn't have to show what was behind the door.

544
00:21:51.920 --> 00:21:52.260
Oh.

545
00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:55.200
You will see what's behind the door at the end, though.

546
00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:55.900
Movie magic.

547
00:21:56.680 --> 00:22:00.920
So Gabe is going to walk Michael through all the toppings for the make-your-own-pizza bar.

548
00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:03.720
There's a really fun thing in deleted scenes, Jenna.

549
00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:04.740
What is it?

550
00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:07.180
Michael wants his own ingredients for the pizza.

551
00:22:07.300 --> 00:22:11.300
He doesn't like all of these amazing ingredients that Gabe has laid out so nicely.

552
00:22:11.800 --> 00:22:15.680
He goes into Gabe's pantry, and he picks out SpaghettiOs.

553
00:22:16.280 --> 00:22:19.900
And Gabe says, I was saving that, you know, for one of the children I mentor.

554
00:22:20.320 --> 00:22:21.780
I remember shooting this.

555
00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:22.240
Yeah.

556
00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:28.120
So I remember, because I was, I remember being worried for Steve.

557
00:22:28.460 --> 00:22:34.180
So Michael makes a pizza with SpaghettiOs as the topping, and he takes this huge bite.

558
00:22:34.300 --> 00:22:35.360
I remember this.

559
00:22:35.440 --> 00:22:35.620
Yes.

560
00:22:35.980 --> 00:22:39.620
Steve had to take a huge bite out of this pizza topped with SpaghettiOs.

561
00:22:39.740 --> 00:22:40.780
It looks so disgusting.

562
00:22:40.780 --> 00:22:42.200
I thought he was going to gag.

563
00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:45.460
And then as Michael, he spits it out into the trash.

564
00:22:45.860 --> 00:22:47.560
It looks so nasty.

565
00:22:47.560 --> 00:22:50.340
It led me down a pizza rabbit hole.

566
00:22:51.080 --> 00:22:52.700
What's a pizza rabbit hole?

567
00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:56.340
Pizza rabbit hole is, and I barely scratched the surface.

568
00:22:56.660 --> 00:23:02.360
But here are a few things I found about how people eat pizza around the world.

569
00:23:02.740 --> 00:23:03.780
What are they?

570
00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:05.340
Let's start with Australia.

571
00:23:06.160 --> 00:23:11.280
There is a Aussie pizza that is topped with barbecue sauce.

572
00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:12.220
That's the base.

573
00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:12.600
Yep.

574
00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:18.000
Mozzarella, cheese, little bits of chopped bacon, sometimes ham, and a few eggs.

575
00:23:18.700 --> 00:23:19.020
Whoa.

576
00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:21.140
The last one surprised me.

577
00:23:21.320 --> 00:23:22.820
What about barbecue sauce, though?

578
00:23:22.960 --> 00:23:23.860
No tomato sauce.

579
00:23:24.040 --> 00:23:27.440
Well, I often eat a, like, barbecue chicken pizza.

580
00:23:27.620 --> 00:23:27.880
Oh.

581
00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:28.600
Well, there you go.

582
00:23:28.600 --> 00:23:29.400
And it's very good.

583
00:23:29.580 --> 00:23:32.240
So why was I so surprised by barbecue sauce?

584
00:23:32.240 --> 00:23:34.500
I don't know, but I would eat that Australian pizza.

585
00:23:34.640 --> 00:23:35.420
With the egg on it?

586
00:23:35.500 --> 00:23:35.920
I would.

587
00:23:36.080 --> 00:23:36.260
Okay.

588
00:23:36.740 --> 00:23:37.600
Would you eat this one?

589
00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:44.360
In Russia, there is a popular pizza known as the makba, if I'm saying that correctly.

590
00:23:44.680 --> 00:23:46.380
It contains the following.

591
00:23:47.040 --> 00:23:50.520
Sardines, tuna, mackerel, salmon, onions.

592
00:23:50.840 --> 00:23:53.020
And it's traditionally served cold.

593
00:23:53.440 --> 00:23:54.000
No.

594
00:23:54.360 --> 00:23:55.360
Pass on makba.

595
00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:58.600
Finland has a funny pizza topping story.

596
00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:01.140
They have a pizza called pizza burlesconi.

597
00:24:01.140 --> 00:24:03.420
And I'll tell you how this pizza came about.

598
00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:09.600
I guess there was an Italian prime minister named Silvio burlesconi, and he made a jab at

599
00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:10.520
Finnish cuisine.

600
00:24:10.800 --> 00:24:11.140
Oh.

601
00:24:11.780 --> 00:24:16.200
He said the Finnish food usually consists of marinated reindeer.

602
00:24:17.240 --> 00:24:19.800
Well, guess what the Finnish did?

603
00:24:20.140 --> 00:24:22.460
Did they put marinated reindeer on a pizza?

604
00:24:22.460 --> 00:24:29.160
They named a pizza after him, and they included a bunch of Finnish stereotypical food, including

605
00:24:29.160 --> 00:24:31.320
smoked reindeer, mushrooms, and red onions.

606
00:24:31.600 --> 00:24:36.760
And this pizza is now served in restaurants across the country and is called the pizza burlesconi.

607
00:24:37.280 --> 00:24:38.440
I would try it.

608
00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:44.500
I might feel bad about eating reindeer because it makes me think of Rudolph, but I guess I'd

609
00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:44.980
try it.

610
00:24:45.080 --> 00:24:45.420
Mm-hmm.

611
00:24:45.600 --> 00:24:46.780
I think I would try it.

612
00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:47.300
Okay.

613
00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:51.980
Here's just a fun tidbit, a pizza tidbit.

614
00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:55.620
Every pizza in Sweden is served with pizza salad.

615
00:24:55.880 --> 00:24:58.640
It's a tangy coleslaw salad with red pepper.

616
00:24:58.900 --> 00:25:00.620
Some people eat it as a side.

617
00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:02.340
Others eat it on top of the pizza.

618
00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:02.840
Mm.

619
00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:06.260
I would eat that, and I would put it on top of my pizza.

620
00:25:06.900 --> 00:25:07.240
Oh.

621
00:25:07.500 --> 00:25:07.760
Okay.

622
00:25:08.080 --> 00:25:10.460
California Pizza Kitchen does a pizza.

623
00:25:10.460 --> 00:25:15.080
They do a BLT pizza, and there's like kind of a salad on top of it.

624
00:25:15.180 --> 00:25:15.900
It's really good.

625
00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:17.980
Quick pizza fun fact.

626
00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:22.080
Norwegians are said to consume the most pizza per capita in the world.

627
00:25:22.180 --> 00:25:22.960
Not America?

628
00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:24.600
Norwegians.

629
00:25:24.800 --> 00:25:25.180
What?

630
00:25:25.420 --> 00:25:25.900
I know.

631
00:25:26.240 --> 00:25:28.280
New Yorkers, you're letting us down.

632
00:25:28.280 --> 00:25:28.900
I know.

633
00:25:29.460 --> 00:25:36.780
Lastly, according to the internet, Korea might be the most adventurous pizza makers out there.

634
00:25:36.900 --> 00:25:37.200
Why?

635
00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:39.380
Basically, anything goes.

636
00:25:40.160 --> 00:25:46.320
Toppings on pizzas can range from kimchi to snails, potatoes, T-bone steak, and calamari.

637
00:25:46.660 --> 00:25:49.060
Some pizzas even have stuffed cruSt.

638
00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:50.840
But guess what it's stuffed with?

639
00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:51.500
What?

640
00:25:51.980 --> 00:25:52.740
Sweet potato.

641
00:25:53.020 --> 00:25:53.200
Oh.

642
00:25:53.820 --> 00:25:54.020
Hmm.

643
00:25:54.500 --> 00:25:55.480
There you have it, guys.

644
00:25:55.660 --> 00:25:57.340
That's just scratching the surface.

645
00:25:57.520 --> 00:25:59.640
There's a lot of pizza content out there.

646
00:25:59.800 --> 00:26:00.840
I want you to know.

647
00:26:00.980 --> 00:26:03.300
Have you ever had a St. Louis-style pizza, Ange?

648
00:26:03.300 --> 00:26:06.340
I really wish I'd brought one in today because it's so on theme.

649
00:26:06.640 --> 00:26:08.580
No, I've never had St. Louis-style pizza.

650
00:26:08.580 --> 00:26:09.120
What is that?

651
00:26:09.120 --> 00:26:09.560
Okay.

652
00:26:10.100 --> 00:26:11.300
We have a pizza place.

653
00:26:11.420 --> 00:26:12.560
It's called Emo's Pizza.

654
00:26:13.140 --> 00:26:17.920
They're most famous for serving the St. Louis-style pizza, but other places do it as well in St. Louis.

655
00:26:18.120 --> 00:26:20.160
It's a very, very thin cruSt.

656
00:26:20.340 --> 00:26:20.620
And it's crispy.

657
00:26:20.620 --> 00:26:21.560
Ooh, I love thin cruSt.

658
00:26:21.720 --> 00:26:22.500
Ooh, I love that.

659
00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:24.120
So, when you bite it, it's crispy.

660
00:26:24.360 --> 00:26:24.800
Ooh.

661
00:26:25.480 --> 00:26:34.840
But the topping is sort of a cheesy sauce that then hardens into a kind of a plastic.

662
00:26:35.620 --> 00:26:36.180
Plastic?

663
00:26:36.340 --> 00:26:37.460
This isn't selling it.

664
00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:41.820
Well, it's made out of a made-up cheese called Provel.

665
00:26:43.460 --> 00:26:45.100
So, it's not real cheese?

666
00:26:45.480 --> 00:26:48.440
No, it's in like sort of the Velveeta category.

667
00:26:48.700 --> 00:26:48.940
Okay.

668
00:26:49.080 --> 00:26:52.020
You know, it's a processed kind of made-up cheese.

669
00:26:52.280 --> 00:26:52.500
Okay.

670
00:26:52.500 --> 00:26:54.540
So, St. Louis-style pizza.

671
00:26:54.720 --> 00:26:58.540
And then you put the other traditional toppings on, sausage, pepperoni, whatever you want.

672
00:26:58.660 --> 00:27:03.260
But it's the crispy, crispy crust with this Provel cheese on top.

673
00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:06.680
So, BJ Novak went to St. Louis to do some stand-up.

674
00:27:06.800 --> 00:27:10.400
And he was like, tell me about your local cuisine.

675
00:27:10.680 --> 00:27:11.460
Where should I go?

676
00:27:11.620 --> 00:27:11.760
Yeah.

677
00:27:11.900 --> 00:27:14.280
And I said, oh, you have to get toasted ravioli.

678
00:27:14.800 --> 00:27:18.060
You have to probably go get some Tedru's frozen custard.

679
00:27:18.200 --> 00:27:22.220
And then you've got to try a St. Louis-style Emo's pizza.

680
00:27:23.100 --> 00:27:26.620
Still to this day, he gives me trouble about this pizza.

681
00:27:26.820 --> 00:27:30.820
Anybody who's not local St. Louis does not appreciate this pizza.

682
00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:31.580
It's interesting.

683
00:27:31.580 --> 00:27:34.000
BJ reached out to me when he was filming in New Orleans.

684
00:27:34.080 --> 00:27:36.500
And I told him where to go to get good gumbo.

685
00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:37.540
Oh, yeah.

686
00:27:37.820 --> 00:27:38.680
He's really cool.

687
00:27:38.820 --> 00:27:41.600
He likes to eat like the local cuisine when he goes places.

688
00:27:41.940 --> 00:27:43.300
But, oh, shoot.

689
00:27:43.440 --> 00:27:44.520
What a missed opportunity.

690
00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:47.120
I could be eating some Emo's pizza right now.

691
00:27:47.240 --> 00:27:48.500
I'd be so happy about it.

692
00:27:49.400 --> 00:27:53.840
Well, next up, Michael's going to have this talking head where he's kind of, I don't know.

693
00:27:54.100 --> 00:27:59.560
He's going on and on about how Gabe likes to entertain versus how he likes to spend an evening.

694
00:27:59.820 --> 00:28:01.360
It was total sad sack.

695
00:28:01.580 --> 00:28:02.340
It was.

696
00:28:02.600 --> 00:28:06.720
He just has like a bag of steamed vegetables from the microwave and a sangria.

697
00:28:07.880 --> 00:28:08.400
Yeah.

698
00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:11.120
Well, we had a really fun fan catch.

699
00:28:11.320 --> 00:28:11.740
Oh, yeah?

700
00:28:11.860 --> 00:28:12.420
Emma R.

701
00:28:12.620 --> 00:28:14.260
in Hoboken, New Jersey, who said,

702
00:28:14.380 --> 00:28:17.940
I noticed that in the background of Michael's talking head at Gabe's apartment,

703
00:28:18.160 --> 00:28:23.220
you can see Oscar out of focus and he's doing what looks like charades.

704
00:28:24.020 --> 00:28:26.760
It starts around five minutes and 17 seconds.

705
00:28:26.760 --> 00:28:30.160
And I think he's acting out the film Back to the Future.

706
00:28:30.480 --> 00:28:31.180
No way.

707
00:28:31.500 --> 00:28:31.720
Yeah.

708
00:28:32.060 --> 00:28:34.340
Emma watched it a couple of times just to be sure.

709
00:28:34.620 --> 00:28:38.480
He only does the first two words, but it really does seem like that's what it might be.

710
00:28:38.480 --> 00:28:41.860
I checked it out and he does.

711
00:28:42.380 --> 00:28:46.200
Oscar does the charade for movie, four words.

712
00:28:46.420 --> 00:28:48.120
First word, back.

713
00:28:48.420 --> 00:28:50.060
And then he does second word.

714
00:28:50.630 --> 00:28:52.820
And then he does like the number two.

715
00:28:53.140 --> 00:28:54.560
But then it cuts off.

716
00:28:54.940 --> 00:28:55.960
Was that in the script?

717
00:28:56.080 --> 00:28:57.600
It was not in the script.

718
00:28:57.840 --> 00:29:01.720
This was just some amazing background performing that we came up with on the day.

719
00:29:02.140 --> 00:29:03.440
But it's really fun to watch.

720
00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:07.380
Well, Michael is going to insist on tossing his own pizza dough.

721
00:29:08.340 --> 00:29:09.640
And there was a blooper.

722
00:29:09.820 --> 00:29:11.400
You know, my daughter loves the bloopers.

723
00:29:11.620 --> 00:29:12.060
Oh, yeah.

724
00:29:12.220 --> 00:29:14.020
And she watched this episode with me and she goes,

725
00:29:14.140 --> 00:29:18.160
Oh, mom, mom, there's this great blooper where Steve is tossing the pizza dough

726
00:29:18.160 --> 00:29:21.640
and it flies up and lands on the boom operator's mic.

727
00:29:21.880 --> 00:29:22.160
Yes.

728
00:29:22.340 --> 00:29:23.780
And so we went and watched it.

729
00:29:23.960 --> 00:29:24.640
It cracked me up.

730
00:29:24.820 --> 00:29:27.060
It was Brian Whittle who was doing the boom for this scene.

731
00:29:27.120 --> 00:29:30.000
And he got a microphone full of pizza dough.

732
00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:37.420
So Randy sent us a really cool video of them testing out this pizza dough hitting the fan bit.

733
00:29:37.640 --> 00:29:38.040
Yeah.

734
00:29:38.420 --> 00:29:40.480
And it was really cool.

735
00:29:40.680 --> 00:29:46.260
You see all these guys, all our crew guys, like just in the warehouse throwing pizza dough at a fan

736
00:29:46.260 --> 00:29:49.080
to see like where does it go when it hits the fan?

737
00:29:49.420 --> 00:29:51.260
Like how fast should the fan be going?

738
00:29:51.580 --> 00:29:52.380
It was cool.

739
00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:56.400
Gabe is going to welcome Pam and Jim into his man cave.

740
00:29:56.400 --> 00:29:59.540
We find out he spent his senior year abroad in Japan.

741
00:30:00.320 --> 00:30:04.780
And also he loves to play some soundscapes on the keyboard.

742
00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:06.140
Yes.

743
00:30:06.400 --> 00:30:09.920
We had a fan catch from Hal C in the UK who said,

744
00:30:10.100 --> 00:30:13.520
Gabe said he spent his year in Japan, yet for the rest of the episode,

745
00:30:13.700 --> 00:30:16.420
everything in his room is from China.

746
00:30:17.760 --> 00:30:18.260
Hmm.

747
00:30:18.680 --> 00:30:19.600
Good catch, Hal.

748
00:30:19.600 --> 00:30:20.220
Nice catch.

749
00:30:20.460 --> 00:30:20.760
Yes.

750
00:30:21.560 --> 00:30:24.840
Also in this scene, you know, Cece is awake.

751
00:30:25.060 --> 00:30:25.960
It's evening.

752
00:30:26.900 --> 00:30:28.340
She's reverse cycling.

753
00:30:28.920 --> 00:30:29.400
Yes.

754
00:30:29.600 --> 00:30:32.260
She's up all night, which means Pam's up all night.

755
00:30:33.100 --> 00:30:34.140
We got a lot of mail.

756
00:30:34.680 --> 00:30:35.480
Did we really?

757
00:30:35.760 --> 00:30:35.940
Yeah.

758
00:30:36.160 --> 00:30:37.020
I want to hear it.

759
00:30:38.040 --> 00:30:41.520
First of all, from Andrea D in Chattanooga, Tennessee.

760
00:30:41.960 --> 00:30:47.120
Why does Pam get upset about Cece waking up at night when she has her out at a party with

761
00:30:47.120 --> 00:30:47.740
the lights on?

762
00:30:48.160 --> 00:30:48.260
Oh!

763
00:30:50.960 --> 00:30:53.400
Caitlin A from Westfield, New Jersey says,

764
00:30:53.580 --> 00:30:55.900
As a mom of three babies under two and a half,

765
00:30:56.020 --> 00:31:00.740
if your baby is reverse cycling or having any type of sleep issue that's causing stress to your

766
00:31:00.740 --> 00:31:04.340
family, why would you bring said baby to an evening party?

767
00:31:04.540 --> 00:31:06.280
We got some fan mail sass!

768
00:31:06.460 --> 00:31:07.200
We did.

769
00:31:07.500 --> 00:31:10.100
Basically, people said they didn't understand the logic.

770
00:31:10.100 --> 00:31:14.140
If she didn't care, they could understand why she brought Cece.

771
00:31:14.360 --> 00:31:17.740
But if she's stressed out about it, why?

772
00:31:17.740 --> 00:31:18.420
Stay home.

773
00:31:18.580 --> 00:31:18.820
Yeah.

774
00:31:19.060 --> 00:31:22.680
And then people wondered, is it because it was her boss's party?

775
00:31:22.680 --> 00:31:24.860
Did she feel obligated to come?

776
00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:25.500
Hmm.

777
00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:27.600
And so, yeah.

778
00:31:28.400 --> 00:31:34.000
I sort of think if it were me, I would have welcomed the excuse to stay home and just

779
00:31:34.000 --> 00:31:36.180
have Jim represent us at the party.

780
00:31:36.300 --> 00:31:36.640
Yeah.

781
00:31:36.800 --> 00:31:38.260
Here's the amazing thing.

782
00:31:38.440 --> 00:31:42.380
